export abstract anchor FactoryBudget:
    budgetReservation:
        Budget enforcement is best effort because provider spend can outrun
        estimates. Where a harness supports a hard per-attempt token or cost
        ceiling, the factory sets one. Where it does not, the engineer records
        the bounded residual exposure they accept.
        A reservation is created atomically with dispatch intent and account
        selection under an account admission lock. Concurrent orders cannot both
        pass admission against the same remaining amount. Reservations are per
        attempt and reconcile with actual usage. If actual exceeds the
        reservation, both are updated before new dispatch. Late readings
        reconcile idempotently.
        A reservation remains a floor until confirmed usage or an explicit
        engineer write-off. Expiry never frees budget for an attempt whose usage
        is unknown; a cautious estimate applies above the retained floor. The
        default expiry for an unconfirmed launch that never started is one hour,
        and the proof required is adapter discovery or negative confirmation.
        Subscription allowances are admission-controlled in usage units. The
        factory tracks unit reservations, model-to-unit conversion, and reset
        windows, and distinguishes throttling from exhausted allowance.
        Concurrent dispatch cannot exceed the allowance by reservation. Variance
        in conversion uses a conservative fallback.
        Budget exhaustion stops API-funded dispatch only. The factory first
        tries another approved account or subscription. Subscription, local, and
        control-plane operations continue under a reserved control budget for
        status, cancellation, assessment, and checks. A large low-priority
        reservation cannot hold budget or block a small high-priority step;
        admission is priority-aware and made at dispatch time.
        On overrun the factory stops new API dispatch and reports confirmed
        spend, reservations, unreported estimates, reading age, and the exact
        limit, then asks the engineer. Running work may continue. When projected
        overrun would exceed the accepted exposure bound, the factory requests
        cancellation; the provider may ignore it. The bound is derived per
        account from concurrent attempt timeouts, maximum burn rate, and
        detection and cancellation latency. A provider-enforced ceiling is
        preferred and treated as hard when available. A best-effort account
        asserts that dispatch stops and the ledger reconciles, not that provider
        spend stays below the bound. No budget is borrowed and no funds are
        assumed. Unresolved attempts keep a quarantine concurrency slot and
        their reservation floors. The factory tracks aggregate unresolved
        exposure per account in estimated currency and usage units, not attempt
        count. When that exposure crosses the approved bound, new launches on
        that account stop and escalate; other accounts and unrelated orders
        continue. Status reports the ledger, window, reading age, enforcement
        mode, and unresolved exposure. Per-order and factory-wide caps remain
        future work.
    accountingModel:
        Each ledger is keyed by account, billing mode, and reset-window epoch
        and holds one scalar unit: currency for an API account or normalized
        usage for a subscription. A subscription allowance is converted through
        the same versioned table as reservations, and the table version is
        pinned for the life of a reservation. A table miss is budget-unknown.
        Build and test compute uses a separate compute ledger; capacity alone is
        not currency. A paid fallback uses a distinct API account or billing-
        mode ledger, never the subscription ledger.
    usageAttribution:
        Admission total is confirmed high-water plus active reservations not yet
        covered by observed advance plus unresolved exposure plus projected
        overrun of active attempts plus an unreported-burn reserve. The reserve
        is added once per account for the stale interval, not per admission.
        A reservation decreases by observed advance since its dispatch baseline.
        Advance allocation goes to the oldest unresolved amount first, then to
        active reservations; partial coverage leaves the remainder unresolved.
        Readings carry a provider identity or per-account sequence and apply only
        when they advance the high-water mark.
    resetWindows:
        Each subscription counter has a window epoch. An attempt holds its
        reservation in every window it spans until the attempt closes or
        confirmed usage arrives; a reset confirmation alone never releases it.
        A pre-reset tail without a final reading is unresolved exposure bounded
        by the burn-rate estimate. A reset requires a provider marker or the
        window boundary; a downward correction is not a reset.
    throttlingClassification:
        A 429 with an explicit exhausted discriminator is a budget blocker. A
        429 with a throttling discriminator uses retry and backoff and may
        trigger the paid-fallback decision. An ambiguous refusal with stale data
        is throttling with an immediate refresh; repeated unexplained refusals
        escalate. A pre-approved fallback policy is scoped per account, order,
        cap, and expiry, and retains the subscription reservation until handoff
        is confirmed.
